<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Implication</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">The transaction extends provider verti=
cal integration beyond regional ambulatory joint ventures. HealthSpan gains a=
 national platform spanning orthopedics, spine, cardiology, gastroenterology =
and ophthalmology, giving it more exposure to the continued shift of procedur=
es away from hospital outpatient departments.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">For payers, the economic question is w=
hether national ownership preserves the lower unit cost of independent ASCs o=
r creates additional contracting leverage for the affiliated health system. F=
or technology vendors, a privately held national platform creates opportuniti=
es for standardized RCM, scheduling and supply-chain deployment, alongside gr=
eater point-solution displacement risk.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Watch next</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
