<h3 style=3D"font-family:Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;font-size:17px;col=
or:#12395b;margin:22px 0 8px;"><a id=3D"story-4" name=3D"story-4" style=3D"co=
lor:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;"></a>4. ONC preserves prior-authorizat=
ion APIs while removing broader certification burden</h3>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>What happened</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">ONC=E2=80=99s final HTI-5 rule removes=
 14 of 60 health-IT certification criteria, revises seven and leaves 39 uncha=
nged. The retained criteria include the electronic prior-authorization APIs a=
nd real-time prescription-benefit capabilities finalized under HTI-4. ONC als=
o reduced real-world testing and Insights reporting obligations and removed m=
odel-card source-attribute requirements from the decision-support-interventio=
ns criterion. The agency estimates $1.55 billion in present-value savings and=
 $108.1 million in annualized savings; most provisions take effect December 2=
8. (<a href=3D"https://healthit.gov/resources/health-data-technology-and-inte=
roperability-onc-deregulatory-actions-to-unleash-prosperity-hti-5-final-rule/=
" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">ONC, <em>HTI-5 Final Rul=
e Overview</em>, October 26, 2026</a>; <a href=3D"https://www.federalregister=
.gov/documents/2026/10/28/2026-21943/health-data-technology-and-interoperabil=
ity-onc-deregulatory-actions-to-unleash-prosperity" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;te=
xt-decoration:underline;">Federal Register, <em>HTI-5 Final Rule</em>, Octobe=
r 28, 2026</a>)</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Implication</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">The rule lowers compliance costs for c=
ertified developers without weakening the immediate electronic prior-authoriz=
ation implementation thesis. Payers, EHR vendors and providers still need the=
 FHIR workflows already scheduled for 2027.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">The trade-off shifts toward buyer dili=
gence. Providers may need to validate functionality contractually where feder=
al certification no longer supplies the same floor, particularly for decision=
-support documentation, testing and older exchange methods. ONC acknowledged =
that downstream end-user costs were not fully quantified.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Watch next</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Whether ONC separately finalizes the i=
nformation-blocking proposals it left under consideration and how buyers adju=
st AI-governance requirements after the model-card rollback.</p>
